// smoke — the second kernel: fields IN. A plume that leans away from a wind.
//
// ⚠ Nothing in this file makes anything look like smoke — no size growth, no
// opacity ramp, no thinning. This is `embers.rill` plus a lean, and the LOOK
// is the spray's appearance. What it demonstrates is a kernel reading a FIELD
// that another caster writes.
//
// The spray's ^ declares `samples $wind cell 0.5`; every tick the wind's
// deposit bag is rasterised onto a lattice over the spray's bounds, and
// `$wind grad at row.pos` reads the gradient there — pointing TOWARD the
// wind's source. So `lean` must be NEGATIVE to push the column downwind; a
// positive one sucks it into the fan. The column straightens when the caster
// is unmounted and its deposit has decayed.
//
// The cast the other way — `casts $dankness` — is declared on the ^spray too:
// one aggregate per tick, not a row's business.
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/smoke.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.gravity =<cells/s²>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.lean    =<NEGATIVE — downwind>
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
describe smoke
    "A plume that leans off a wind: every row reads the wind where it is and gets pushed downwind of it, so the column bends for as long as the fan blows and straightens again once it stops."

spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.k.gravity
$wind grad at row.pos | mul plane.drift.@self.k.lean | write row.vel add
perish
